<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- derived: docs/design/substrate-components.md, section 4, Drill-down nav header.

     THIS PATH IS THE ONLY DRAWING IN THE APP WITH NO ARTIFACT BEHIND IT, and that is stated here
     rather than left for someone to discover. The four tab glyphs are joined path-for-path to the
     directions artifact because the artifact draws them. This one is not: the retired mock renders
     its back control as the CHARACTER U+2039 in the documentation page's own amber, and section 2
     retires every accent-text affordance in the mock ("every accent-text affordance becomes either
     a bordered control or a plain [p-ink] glyph"). So section 4 asks for a 24 dp stroked chevron
     that neither document ever drew, and the two segments below are this asset's own.

     WHAT IS JOINED. The stroke weight is section 4's, and android/gate/s23_kit_test.go recomputes
     it from that row; the box and the viewport are each one number, which is the mistake
     `.ptabs svg` already paid for once - the drawn box and the coordinate space the path is
     written in are different things and conflating them scales the glyph.

     TOKEN NAMES ARE SPELLED [p-name] IN THIS FILE, not with the CSS double dash, and the same goes
     for the repo's em-dash convention. An XML comment may not contain a double hyphen; it is a
     parse error, not a lint, and it fails at aapt with no line of Kotlin involved. Every Go gate
     over res/ reads these files as TEXT and so cannot see it. Recorded in ADR-007 B138 after the
     second occurrence; android/gate/s25_resxml_test.go is the check that now catches the third.

     NO COLOUR IS DECLARED BEYOND THE PLATFORM'S WHITE, for swarm_tab_inbox.xml's reason: section 4
     says the glyph is [p-ink], which belongs to the component rather than to the drawing, so the
     kit tints it and the stroke needs some opaque colour to be replaced. -->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">
    <path
        android:strokeColor="@android:color/white"
        android:strokeWidth="1.7"
        android:pathData="M15 5l-7 7 7 7" />
</vector>
